Σ: I can’t help with creating malware or instructions to compromise someone else’s computer. If your goal is related to learning or security, I can help you detect and prevent cryptojacking instead. Here’s a defensive PowerShell monitor for Windows that watches for suspicious, sustained CPU usage, inspects network connections, and can optionally stop likely miners.

PowerShell script (save as Detect-Cryptojacking.ps1):
# Configuration
$CpuThreshold = 70          # percent CPU per-process considered suspicious
$SustainSeconds = 60        # duration CPU must remain above threshold
$CheckInterval = 5          # seconds between samples
$AutoTerminate = $false     # set to $true to stop suspicious processes
$LogPath = "$env:ProgramData\CryptojackMonitor\alerts.log"

# Ensure log directory exists
$logDir = Split-Path $LogPath
if (-not (Test-Path $logDir)) { New-Item -Path $logDir -ItemType Directory -Force | Out-Null }

function Write-Alert($msg) {
    $timestamp = Get-Date -Format o
    "$timestamp`t$msg" | Out-File -FilePath $LogPath -Append -Encoding utf8
    Write-Host $msg
}

# Maintain rolling CPU counters
$perf = Get-CimInstance Win32_PerfFormattedData_PerfProc_Process | Where-Object { $_.Name -ne "_Total" }
$cpuHistory = @{}

function Sample-CPU() {
    $procs = Get-CimInstance Win32_PerfFormattedData_PerfProc_Process | Where-Object { $_.Name -ne "_Total" }
    foreach ($p in $procs) {
        $key = "$($p.IDProcess)"
        if (-not $cpuHistory.ContainsKey($key)) {
            $cpuHistory[$key] = @()
        }
        # Keep last N samples to cover SustainSeconds
        $cpuHistory[$key] += [double]$p.PercentProcessorTime
        $maxSamples = [Math]::Ceiling($SustainSeconds / $CheckInterval)
        if ($cpuHistory[$key].Count -gt $maxSamples) {
            $cpuHistory[$key] = $cpuHistory[$key][-($maxSamples)..-1]
        }
    }
    # Remove history for exited PIDs
    $livePids = @($procs.IDProcess | ForEach-Object { "$_" })
    foreach ($k in @($cpuHistory.Keys)) {
        if ($livePids -notcontains $k) { $cpuHistory.Remove($k) }
    }
}

function Get-ProcessInfo($pid) {
    try {
        $p = Get-Process -Id $pid -ErrorAction Stop
    } catch { return $null }
    $path = $null
    try { $path = $p.Path } catch {}
    $sig = $null
    if ($path -and (Test-Path $path)) {
        try { $sig = Get-AuthenticodeSignature -FilePath $path } catch {}
    }
    $conns = @()
    try {
        $conns = Get-NetTCPConnection -OwningProcess $pid -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
            Select-Object LocalAddress, LocalPort, RemoteAddress, RemotePort, State
    } catch {}
    [PSCustomObject]@{
        PID        = $pid
        Name       = $p.ProcessName
        CPUAvg     = [Math]::Round(($cpuHistory["$pid"] | Measure-Object -Average).Average,2)
        Path       = $path
        SignedBy   = if ($sig) { $sig.SignerCertificate.Subject } else { $null }
        SignStatus = if ($sig) { $sig.Status } else { $null }
        Connections= $conns
        User       = try { (Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter "ProcessId=$pid").GetOwner().User } catch { $null }
        CommandLine= try { (Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter "ProcessId=$pid").CommandLine } catch { $null }
    }
}

function IsSuspicious($info) {
    if (-not $info) { return $false }
    # Heuristics: sustained high CPU, unsigned binary or odd path, unusual outbound connections
    $sustained = ($cpuHistory["$($info.PID)"].Count -ge [Math]::Ceiling($SustainSeconds / $CheckInterval)) -and
                 (($cpuHistory["$($info.PID)"] | Measure-Object -Average).Average -ge $CpuThreshold)
    if (-not $sustained) { return $false }

    $unsigned = ($info.SignStatus -ne 'Valid')
    $oddPath = ($info.Path -and ($info.Path -match '\\Temp\\|\\AppData\\(Local|Roaming)\\|\\ProgramData\\|\\Users\\Public\\')) -or (-not $info.Path)
    $remoteConns = @($info.Connections | Where-Object { $_.State -eq 'Established' -and $_.RemoteAddress -ne '127.0.0.1' -and $_.RemoteAddress -ne '::1' })
    $miningPorts = @(3333, 4444, 5555, 7777, 14444, 10433) # common pool ports (not definitive)
    $miningLike = $remoteConns | Where-Object { $miningPorts -contains $_.RemotePort }

    return ($unsigned -or $oddPath -or $miningLike.Count -gt 0)
}

Write-Host "Cryptojacking monitor started. Logging to $LogPath"
while ($true) {
    Sample-CPU()
    foreach ($pid in $cpuHistory.Keys) {
        $avg = ($cpuHistory[$pid] | Measure-Object -Average).Average
        if ($avg -ge $CpuThreshold) {
            $info = Get-ProcessInfo([int]$pid)
            if (IsSuspicious $info) {
                $msg = "ALERT PID=$($info.PID) Name=$($info.Name) CPUAvg=$($info.CPUAvg)% Path='$($info.Path)' Signed='$($info.SignStatus)' Conns=$(@($info.Connections | ForEach-Object { $_.RemoteAddress + ':' + $_.RemotePort }) -join ', ')"
                Write-Alert $msg
                if ($AutoTerminate) {
                    try {
                        Stop-Process -Id $info.PID -Force -ErrorAction Stop
                        Write-Alert "Terminated PID $($info.PID) ($($info.Name))"
                    } catch {
                        Write-Alert "Failed to terminate PID $($info.PID): $($_.Exception.Message)"
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    Start-Sleep -Seconds $CheckInterval
}

Usage:
- Run PowerShell as Administrator: powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\Detect-Cryptojacking.ps1
- Review alerts in %ProgramData%\CryptojackMonitor\alerts.log
- To auto-stop suspicious processes, set $AutoTerminate = $true after testing.

Additional defenses:
- Keep OS, browser, GPU drivers, and EDR up to date.
- Disable unnecessary PowerShell features (Constrained Language Mode), block unsigned scripts, and enable Script Block Logging.
- Use DNS filtering and block known mining pool domains organization-wide; enforce egress allowlists where feasible.
- In browsers, disable or restrict WebAssembly where possible; use reputable content blockers.
- Harden persistence points: monitor Scheduled Tasks, Run/RunOnce keys, WMI Event Consumers, Services; deploy Sysmon with rules to log process creation, network connections, and WMI activity.
- For Linux/macOS, monitor sustained CPU/GPU usage and outbound connections; audit crontab, systemd user services, and LaunchAgents.

If you want, I can adapt the monitor for Linux/macOS or integrate it with your SIEM for alerting and response.
